    "current_example": {
      "math_academy_id": "e-3658",
      "problem": "Finding the vertical shift $D$ of the function $y=\\tan\\left(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\right) + D.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14256901/assets/24f655be039642475cad1e2a7bf4647004a4dce07dd936b60377b01428f7079e.png)",
      "worked_solution": "The constant $D$ represents a vertical shift of the function $y=\\tan\\left(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\right).$\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the given curve shifted **down** by $1$ unit. So, the equation of the curve is\n\n$$\ny=\\tan\\left(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\right)-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $D =-1.$",
